- op: discuss  # ac71
  at: '2026-03-20T15:33:44Z'  # ac71
  by: agent  # ac71
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ac71
  clock: 7  # ac71
  nonce: ac71  # ac71
  message: "## Plain-language explanation\n\nWhen hypergumbo analyzes a C or Java codebase, it builds a call graph showing which functions call which other functions. But it only creates edges between functions that *exist in the project's source code*. Standard library functions like fopen() (C) or InputStream.read() (Java) are provided by the language runtime, not defined in the project — so they never appear as nodes in the graph.\n\nThe IO boundary feature works by scanning call edges and matching callee names against a catalog of known IO functions (fopen = file read, socket = network, etc.). Since stdlib calls don't appear as edges, the catalog has nothing to match against, and the IO boundary map comes back empty.\n\n## What's affected\n\n- **C repos**: minisign (0 IO edges), git-crypt (4 subprocess edges only, no file IO), tmux (0 IO edges). All use fopen/fread/fwrite extensively.\n- **Java repos**: jedis (0 IO edges), commons-compress (0 IO edges), gradle (0 IO edges). All use InputStream/OutputStream extensively.\n- **C++ repos**: Same issue via the C alias. git-crypt gets partial results because it also uses execvp/wait which are project-resolvable.\n\nThat's 7 of 84 repos tested (8%) with completely broken IO boundary detection.\n\n## Why it's not a simple fix\n\nThe current design is intentional — only tracking intra-project calls keeps the graph focused and avoids thousands of edges to stdlib functions that every file calls (malloc, free, printf, etc.). The two options are:\n\n1. **Create unresolved-external-call edges** for all stdlib calls. Simple to implement but inflates the graph significantly (every C file would gain dozens of edges to stdlib). Would need filtering to keep the graph useful.\n\n2. **Have io-boundaries scan the AST directly** for IO function call patterns, bypassing the call graph entirely. Scoped to IO detection, doesn't affect the main graph. More work but cleaner separation of concerns.\n\n## Stakes\n\nFor repos where this matters (C system tools, Java servers), the IO boundary and verify-claims features are non-functional. This limits hypergumbo's value proposition for security auditing of C/Java codebases — exactly the repos where IO boundary analysis would be most useful (minisign is a signing tool, jedis is a Redis client doing socket IO)."  # ac71
- op: discuss  # c861
  at: '2026-03-20T15:38:34Z'  # c861
  by: human  # c861
  actor: jgstern  # c861
  clock: 8  # c861
