  message: "why is this a nonissue for python? and I thought this sort of thing (calls to the standard library or some external library) is why we created the whole supply-chain tier classification."  # c861
- op: discuss  # f7ac
  at: '2026-03-20T15:43:19Z'  # f7ac
  by: agent  # f7ac
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f7ac
  clock: 9  # f7ac
  nonce: f7ac  # f7ac
  message: "Great questions — both point to the same underlying design tension.\n\n## Why Python doesn't have this problem\n\nPython's analyzer creates call edges for *all* function calls it encounters, including calls to functions that aren't defined in the project. When Python code calls `open('file.txt')` or `os.path.exists(path)`, those appear as call edges to unresolved symbols like `python:builtins:0-0:open:function`. The IO catalog then matches against these unresolved edges and correctly tags them.\n\nThe Go, C, and Java analyzers took a different design path: they only create edges to functions with symbols in the project registry. The reasoning was to avoid graph pollution — C code calls malloc/free/printf on nearly every line, and including all of those as edges would massively inflate the graph.\n\nSo it's a design split across analyzers, not a fundamental limitation.\n\n## The supply-chain tier connection\n\nYou're right that this is exactly what supply-chain tiers were designed to address. Tier 3 (external_dep) and tier 4 (transitive_dep) exist precisely to represent stdlib and third-party code. The problem is that for C/Java, those external symbols never get *created as nodes* in the first place — they exist only as unresolved stubs with no call edges pointing at them.\n\nThe fix that aligns best with the existing architecture would be option 2 from before, reframed: **create lightweight external-call nodes at tier 3/4 for stdlib calls, but only for functions that match the IO primitive catalog**. This is scoped (only IO-relevant stdlib functions get nodes, not every malloc), uses the supply chain classification as intended, and gives the IO boundary scanner edges to match against.\n\nThis avoids the graph inflation concern because we're only creating nodes for ~200 catalog-listed IO functions per language, not the full stdlib surface. And it makes the tier classification actually useful for C/Java repos — currently those repos show 0% tier 3/4 which is misleading."  # f7ac
