  message: "but why not just have *all* 100+ language analyzers create edges for all symbols they encounter, the way Python does, and then classify edges by supply-chain tier? isn't that what Python analyzer does? if it works for the Python analyzer why can't it work for everything?"  # a212
- op: discuss  # cd2d
  at: '2026-03-20T15:54:02Z'  # cd2d
  by: agent  # cd2d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # cd2d
  clock: 11  # cd2d
  nonce: cd2d  # cd2d
  message: "You're right — that's the cleanest approach and I was overcomplicating it.\n\nThe Python analyzer creates edges for every call it encounters. When it can't resolve the callee to a project symbol, it creates an edge to an unresolved node (e.g., `python:builtins:0-0:open:function`). These unresolved nodes naturally fall into tier 3/4 in supply-chain classification. The graph stays navigable because the tier system lets you filter them out when you want a focused view.\n\nThere's no fundamental reason the other analyzers can't do the same. The Go, C, Java, Rust, etc. analyzers all *detect* these calls in the AST — they just discard them when the callee isn't in the project registry. The fix is to stop discarding and instead emit an unresolved-external edge, same as Python does.\n\nThe original concern was graph inflation (C code calls malloc/free/printf everywhere), but that's what tier filtering is for — if you want a clean first-party view, slice with `--max-tier 1`. The full graph has the external calls available for IO boundary analysis and security auditing.\n\nI'll reopen this as `todo_hard` and implement the change across analyzers. The pattern is the same for each: where the analyzer currently does `if not resolved: skip`, change it to `if not resolved: emit unresolved edge`. Python already proves it works."  # cd2d
